   "type": "done",
   "answer": "Aligned, contiguous accesses generate fewer transactions because the GPU memory coalescer groups addresses by cache-sector or transaction granularity before accessing DRAM [S4, p. 47]. When addresses are aligned and contiguous, they touch fewer memory segments, allowing the coalescer to pack them into fewer transactions [S1, p. 48].\n\nIn contrast, strided accesses can touch many more segments because the addresses are spread out across the memory space [S1, p. 48]. Additionally, misalignment can cause accesses to cross extra boundaries, further increasing the number of transactions required [S1, p. 48].\n\n**Reasoning Summary:**\n1.  **Coalescing Mechanism:** The coalescer evaluates a warp's memory instruction and groups addresses based on granularity (e.g., cache sectors) [S4, p. 47].\n2.  **Contiguous Access:** Aligned, contiguous addresses fall within fewer of these segments, resulting in fewer distinct transactions to service [S1, p. 48].\n3.  **Strided Access:** Strided addresses are spaced apart, causing them to span many more segments. Each segment touched typically requires a separate transaction, leading to a higher total transaction count [S1, p. 48].\n\n**Citations:**\n*   [S1] Page 48: \"Coalescing is evaluated for one warp memory instruction. Aligned, contiguous addresses touch fewer segments. Misalignment can cross extra boundaries. Strided accesses can touch many more segments.\"\n*   [S4] Page 47: \"The coalescer groups addresses by cache-sector or transaction granularity before DRAM access. The transaction count depends on alignment, address spacing, access width, and GPU architecture.\"",
